[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router4
diagnosis_text:
Location C’s default gateway (router4) is isolated from the rest of the network because both of its uplink interfaces are down. On router4, eth4 (192.168.15.2/24, link to router5) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24, link to router3) are administratively/down at the OS level. Evidence: router4 ip_addr shows eth4 and eth5 state DOWN, while only eth0 (192.168.200.1/24) is UP. Consequently, FRR on router4 has no learned routes—frr_show_ip_route shows only the connected 192.168.200.0/24—and router4 responds to Location C hosts with “Destination Net Unreachable” for 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24 (pc11→172.16.10.4 gets ICMP from 192.168.200.1). 

Mechanism: With both uplinks down, router4 cannot form RIP adjacencies to router3/router5 and has no path to Location A. It therefore generates ICMP net-unreachable for those subnets. Other zones still reach each other via router3 and router5, which remain interconnected (their link interfaces are UP), so the outage is localized to Location C.

Localization: router4, interfaces eth4 (to router5 on 192.168.15.0/24) and eth5 (to router3 on 172.25.1.0/24).

Root cause: Both uplink interfaces on router4 were disabled/down, isolating Location C from the core.

Corrective fix: Bring router4’s uplinks back up and restore routing. On router4: ip link set eth4 up; ip link set eth5 up (verify IPs 192.168.15.2/24 and 172.25.1.1/24 remain). Ensure FRR is running so RIP re-converges; then Location C will regain reachability to 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24.
=== END ===